.TH TR2PDF 1
.SH NAME
tr2pdf \- troff's output as PDF
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tr2pdf
[
.BI -p " size"
] [
.BI -t " title"
] [
.BI -w " lwid"
] [
.B -l
] [
.B -n
] [
.BI -F " dir"
] [
.BI -d " x=v"
] [
.B -W
] [
.B -u
] [
.B -c
] [
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tr2pdf
reads the pages
.IR troff (1)
described (its
.I file
or its standard input) and writes them as PDF.
It embeds the fonts, OpenType (Libertinus's CFF) and TrueType, subset
to the glyphs the document uses, compressed, each with a map from its
glyphs to the characters they are, so that a reader can search and copy
the text; a glyph whose font description names no character (beyond
U+FFFF, as a math font's alphabets) is the character its glyph name
says by the Adobe Glyph List's rules
.RB ( u1D465 ,
.BR uni210E ).
It is a translation of Ali Gholami Rudi's Neatpost; with
.B -c
it writes what Neatpost writes.
.PP
Besides the text and drawing, it reads troff's device controls
.RB ( \eX' ... ' ):
.TP
.BI "papersize " w x h
The page's size, in points.
.TP
.BI "pdf " "file height"
Include a page of a PDF.
.TP
.BI "link " "target width height"
A link, from here, to a URI or
.BI # name\fR.
.TP
.BI "dest " "name " \fR[ page \fR]
A destination here (at the page's top, with
.BR page ).
.TP
.BI "outline " "level title"
A bookmark here.
.TP
.BI "folio " f
The page's number as printed: its label.
.TP
.BI "info " "key value"
The PDF's information
.RB ( Title ,
.BR Author ).
.TP
.BI "name " "name page offset"
.TP
.BI "mark " "title page offset level"
Neatpost's: a destination and a bookmark at a page and place given.
.TP
.BI "image " "file w h"
A JPEG or PNG image, its lower left corner here,
.I w
by
.I h
(troff's units; one of them 0, the image's proportions give it): a
JPEG's data as it is, a PNG's decoded and compressed again, its alpha a
soft mask, its palette an indexed colour space (not an interlaced PNG,
which is said).
Each image is one object, however many pages show it.
.TP
.BI "vstretch " w
The space after the line set last may stretch, by
.I w
(troff's units), others in proportion.
.TP
.BI "vfoot " "b vs"
The page's text ends here; a full page's last baseline is at
.IR b ,
its lines
.I vs
apart. The space the page lacks is spread through its text: to the
.B vstretch
points first, each as much as its
.IR w ,
the rest over its lines, so the page ends where a full page does (as
LaTeX's
.BR \eflushbottom ).
What is set after (notes, the folio) does not move; links and
destinations move with the text. A page short by more than five lines
is set as it is.
.TP
.BI "tag " "..."
The document's structure, for a tagged PDF (below).
.TP
.BI "lang " l
The document's language
.RB ( en ,
.BR de-CH ).
.PP
Given
.B tag
controls, the PDF is tagged (ISO 32000-1, 14.7 and 14.8), for those who
hear it read or have it reflowed: its elements, as troff's output gives
them, are its structure, and everything it sets is marked as an
element's or as an artifact:
.TP
.BI "tag push " "S \fR[\fPid\fR] [\fPKey\fB=\fPvalue\fR ...]\fP"
An element of type
.I S
.RB ( BlockQuote ,
.BR Note ,
.BR Formula ,
.BR Table ,
.BR TR ...)
in the one open; a grouping one (as
.BR BlockQuote )
after the block open is closed.
.I Id
opens again the element given it: a note continued on the next page is
one note.
Attributes
.RB ( ColSpan=2 ,
.BR Scope=Column )
are the element's.
.TP
.B "tag pop"
The element pushed last closed, and all open in it.
.TP
.BI "tag close " S
Those open closed, through the innermost
.IR S .
.TP
.BI "tag " "S \fR[\fPid\fR] [\fPKey\fB=\fPvalue\fR ...]\fP"
A block
.RB ( P ,
.BR H1 ,
.BR TOCI ,
.BR TD ...):
the block open closed,
.I S
opened in its place.
.TP
.B "tag end"
The block open closed.
.TP
.B "tag art\fR, \fPtag endart"
What is set between them is an artifact: running heads, folios.
.TP
.BI "tag alt " text
The open element's alternate description (a picture's, an equation's).
.TP
.BI "tag lang " l
The open element's language.
.PP
Text set where only a grouping element is open is a paragraph of its
own; lines drawn are artifacts, but in a
.B Figure
or a
.BR Formula ;
a link is a
.B Link
element, the text in its rectangle its content and, read out, its
annotation's description.
The catalogue says the PDF is tagged, its language, and that a viewer
show its title; its metadata (XMP) has its title and author.
.IR Troff (6)'s
.B -mbook
gives a book's structure;
.IR eqn (1)
and
.IR tbl (1)
with
.B -t
give their equations' and tables'.
.PP
The options:
.TP
.BI -p " size"
The paper
.RB ( letter ,
.BR a4 ,
or its width and height in tenths of a millimetre).
.TP
.BI -t " title"
The title.
.TP
.BI -w " lwid"
Lines' thickness, in thousandths of an em (40).
.TP
.B -l
Landscape.
.TP
.B -n
Draw glyphs by name.
.TP
.BI -F " dir"
The device directory.
.TP
.BI -d " x=v"
Set a device variable.
.TP
.B -W
Embed fonts whole, not subset.
.TP
.B -u
Claim conformance to PDF/UA-1 (ISO 14289-1), in the metadata: for a
document whose structure is given whole, its pictures described.
.TP
.B -c
Write what Neatpost writes (no structure).
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/tr2pdf
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR troff (1),
.IR mkfn (1),
.IR page (1)
